In article <2sqfto$6nq@scunix2.harvard.edu>,
Sung Song <sysong@husc8.harvard.edu> wrote:
>
>I have no access to spoilers,  but my experience tells me differently....
>When  you pray, you must be in "dire straits". That means one (or more) of
>the many following:
>1) low on Hp; 2) deathly sick; 3) lycanthropy; 4) etc....

Not in my experience.  If you pray when nothing's wrong, one of the
following three things will usually happen:

"You feel that <god> is pleased" (if your alignment etc. is OK)
"You feel that <god> is well-pleased" (If same is very good)t
"...well-pleased" and a spoiler about the castle
"...well-pleased" and a spellbook
"You shall be my [Hand of Elbereth/Envoy of Balance/Glory of Arioch]"

I think which result you get is based on alignment+luck+a random
number.  They are in ascending order of difficulty to get.

>If you are wearing a cursed blindfold or a cursed levitation object, your God
>will uncurse them. BUT, he will not always uncurse your cursed items. 
>Consider that your god sometimes restores your ability. Does that mean that
>after getting a message like: "You feel clumsy..." you should pray and expect
>restoration? The logic is that your god will aid you when you require divine
>restitution but he will not be your celestial nurse and pamper you.

Abilities lost to abuse (like when you get the message "You feel
clumsy" and loose dexterity) can't be restored by any means (except
excercising or wearing a magical item that increases the ability in
question).  This has nothing to do with your god's pampering you;  you
are only as likely to get back strength when poison has cost you 5
points as when it has cost you 1.  I could be wrong about this last.
SImilarly, if you abuse strength to the point where it becomes 2, you
can no more expect your god to help you than if you had just lost a
little.

>Thus, my solution would be:
>1) put yourself in a position where the God can legitimately come to your 
>aid. (Eat a rotten corpse.) Pray, and hope that he (or she) will benevolently
>uncurse your objects.

I think that this is actually a bad idea.  When you pray and are in
trouble, your god solves your _worst_ problem, and then _might_ solve
some more.  I think you're more likely to get stuff uncursed if you
pray when otherwise fine.

>2) carry around a blessed scroll of removed curse in a sack.

Or holy water, if this isn't available.



